# Corpus licence

The markdown corpus under `corpus/` mixes content from several upstream sources, each under its own licence. Redistribution follows the upstream terms.

## UK Crown corpus (statute + UK regulator guidance)

Sources: legislation.gov.uk, ICO, NCSC, MHRA, CMA, FCA Handbook.

Licensed under the **Open Government Licence v3.0**. Redistribution requires the attribution statement:

> Contains public sector information licensed under the Open Government Licence v3.0.

Full text: https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/doc/open-government-licence/version/3/

## EU corpus

Sources: EUR-Lex (CELEX-numbered instruments including EU GDPR, EU AI Act, MDR, IVDR, NIS2, PSD2, EAA, Charter of Fundamental Rights).

Redistributed under **Commission Decision 2011/833/EU** terms (free reuse with attribution). Each rendered markdown file carries the source URI in its frontmatter.

Full text: https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/dec/2011/833/oj

## W3C WCAG

Source: w3.org/TR/WCAG22/.

Redistributed under the **W3C Document License**. Verbatim quoting permitted with attribution and the W3C licence notice.

Full text: https://www.w3.org/Consortium/Legal/2015/doc-license

## Proprietary professional and technical standards (Tier C)

Sources: BACP, UKCP, HCPC, BPS, ISO, BSI.

**No verbatim text is redistributed.** Each Tier C file in `corpus/standards/` is a pointer record containing:

- Title and canonical citation reference.
- Source URI.
- A neutral one-sentence description in the corpus author's own words.

This falls within fair-dealing for criticism, review and reporting current events because no substantive copying occurs. Consumers must click through to the source URI to read the standard's text.

## Consumer responsibilities

Anyone forking, mirroring or otherwise redistributing this corpus must:

1. Preserve the OGL3 attribution statement.
2. Preserve EUR-Lex source URIs in frontmatter.
3. Preserve the W3C Document License notice for WCAG content.
4. Maintain the Tier C "external index only" posture for proprietary standards.

The MIT-licensed Python pipeline in `build.py`, `lib/`, and `tests/` carries its own licence (`LICENSE-CODE`) and is reusable independently of the corpus.
